content.text:
Both checks done: `providers-litellm` is at **0.1.1** (already released — normal release-plz flow, no ascend ritual), and the book mentions `ToolCallDelta` only as a variant name with no per-`call_id` name semantics, so this needs no book edit. Conscious call, recorded in the spec.

## Design

**Mechanism.** In `handle_tool_call`, immediately after the `call_id` resolves, rewrite the correlation key to a canonical `Key::Id(call_id)` and migrate any fragments buffered under the pre-canonical key into that slot:

```rust
fn canonicalize(&mut self, key: Key, call_id: &str) -> Key {
    let canonical = Key::Id(call_id.to_owned());
    if key == canonical { return canonical; }
    // Fragments buffered under the pre-canonical key arrived first, so they lead.
    if let Some(old) = self.pending.remove(&key) {
        let slot = self.pending.entry(canonical.clone()).or_default();
        slot.name.insert_str(0, &old.name);
        slot.args.insert_str(0, &old.args);
    }
    // flush_buffered_names and warn_unresolved_pending both resolve a pending
    // key through tool_calls; the canonical key must resolve too.
    self.tool_calls.entry(canonical.clone()).or_insert_with(|| call_id.to_owned());
    canonical
}
```

Everything downstream (`name_emitted`, `warned_late_name`, `pending`, the flush condition) then operates on the canonical key without changing its own type. `tool_calls` keeps the original `Key::Index(i) → call_id` entry so later index-only deltas still resolve.

**Why this is the whole fix.** Canonicalization happens *before* any emission, so `name_emitted` only ever receives canonical keys — one `call_id` has exactly one state entry, and the at-most-one-name invariant holds by construction rather than by guard. The pre-id window is the only remaining `Key::Index` state, and a `Key::Id` entry is unresolvable by definition (it is keyed *by* the id), so at most one Index-keyed buffer ever migrates per call — which makes the prepend unambiguous in wire order.

**Three things fall out for free.** The secondary defect closes: with no losing key, the existing late-name `warn!` fires on its own, so no new `debug!` is needed. Sequence B reassembles to `get_weather`. And `warn_unresolved_pending` stays correct because canonical keys are registered in `tool_calls`.

**Two comments must be rewritten, not left.** `Key`'s `Ord` derive is currently justified as making "the dual-key winner predictable" — that rationale dies, but the sort is still needed for deterministic ordering *between different calls*, so the derive stays with a corrected reason. And `flush_buffered_names`'s `already` guard keeps its code but gets a doc comment saying it is now redundant given canonicalization and why it is kept as a net.

**The `providers-openai` divergence (AC #3).** That translator is structurally immune — `async-openai` 0.41's `ChatCompletionMessageToolCallChunk.index` is a required `u32`, not an `Option`, so there is one key space and no dual-key hazard. But it is not fully aligned either: after this change, litellm merges two *indexes* carrying the same `id` into one call, where openai would emit a name for each. That shape is malformed and unobserved, so I propose documenting the divergence in a comment in `chat.rs` rather than changing its behaviour. Consequence to accept: that comment touches a packaged file, so release-plz will patch-bump `providers-openai` (0.2.22 → 0.2.23) and cascade the facade. Harmless, but it will show up in the release PR.

**Tests** (all in `stream.rs`'s `mod tests`, where SMA-547's dual-key tests already live):

| Test | Asserts | Fails today? |
|---|---|---|
| `dual_key_call_emits_at_most_one_name_mid_stream` | Sequence A through `consume` — exactly one `Some(name)` per `call_id`, **mid-stream** | **yes** — the AC's required failing test |
| `name_fragments_split_across_the_key_boundary_reassemble` | Sequence B yields `get_weather` | **yes** — today yields `weather` |
| `dual_key_late_fragment_is_reported` | `warned_late_name` records the drop in Sequence A | yes |
| `one_call_id_under_two_keys_flushes_a_single_name` | retargeted: now `get_weather`, still one name | assertion updated |
| `flush_does_not_re_emit_a_name_already_flushed_under_another_key` | still passes; docstring corrected to name the real mechanism | no |
| `buffered_args_survive_a_bare_id_delta` | unchanged — pins that migration preserves drain-once args | no |

**Out of scope:** the four non-fragmenting providers; `providers-openai`'s behaviour; SMA-533's suite itself; the `or_insert` policy when one index reports two different ids (pre-existing, unrelated).

Does this look right? If so I'll write it to `docs/superpowers/specs/2026-08-19-sma-550-litellm-dual-key-correlation-design.md`, commit it, and hand it to the adversarial spec-challenger.

